<div class="article-body">

<p>To lower the pH in a hydroponic reservoir, add a food-safe acid in small doses and re-check: about 1 mL per gallon at a time, stir for a minute, wait half an hour, measure again. That is the whole method, and it takes an hour to learn. If you have been doing exactly that and your pH is right back up the next morning, the acid was never your problem. Your water's alkalinity is.</p>

<p>I spent a month learning that the hard way. Five-gallon bucket, four heads of butterhead, one bottle of phosphoric pH down, and a meter that read 5.8 every evening and 6.6 every breakfast. I blamed the bottle. Then I blamed the meter and bought a second one, which is a $22 way of not solving anything.</p>

<p>Here is what I finally saw. I was not dosing pH. I was buying carbon dioxide, one capful at a time.</p>

<p>The dose was working exactly as advertised. It was just not doing the job I thought it was doing. The acid spends itself on something already sitting in your water, that something runs out, and the water drifts back to where it started. Find out what that something is, and the whole problem collapses into one purchase and ten minutes of measuring.</p>

<p>That something is alkalinity, and it lives in your tap water. It has nothing to do with your nutrient bottle.</p>

<h2 id="what">1. What pH Down Actually Does to Your Water</h2>

<p>pH down is acid. That part is obvious, and it is also where most guides stop, which leaves you thinking acid somehow grabs the number and holds it down. It does not. Acid reacts with something already dissolved in your water, and that reaction is what moves the reading.</p>

<p>What it reacts with is bicarbonate, written HCO3 minus. Almost every municipal supply carries it, partly because it comes out of limestone bedrock and partly because water utilities add carbonate on purpose so their pipes do not corrode. When you pour acid in, one bicarbonate ion and one hydrogen ion meet and turn into water plus carbon dioxide. The CO2 leaves the reservoir. Your air stone helps it leave faster.</p>

<p>So the acid is spent. It is not a chemical you add and keep, like nutrients. Every drop you pour is used up on the bicarbonate it can find. Once the bicarbonate runs out, your next drop does something different and much more violent: it drops pH hard, often straight past your target and into the low fours.</p>

<p>This is why the bottle you buy matters more than the bottle tells you. Here is what the common ones actually do once they are in the water.</p>

