<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> the organic acids in that bottom half are not weaker versions of the top half. They are food. Biofilm and algae in a reservoir eat acetate and citrate the same way you eat bread, and while they eat, your pH drifts back up. In a sterile, blacked-out, lab-clean system you might never notice. In a real bucket sitting under a shelf light with warm water in it, you notice inside a day.</p>

<p>That single distinction explains most of the Reddit threads that start with "pH down does not work." Half the time it is a citric acid bottle. The other half, it is the next section.</p>

<h2 id="back">2. Why Your pH Climbs Back: The Alkalinity Sponge</h2>

<p>Picture wringing out a wet sponge. You can get it decently dry with your hands. Then you set it down next to a full sink and walk away, and it soaks itself back up while you are not looking. Alkalinity is the sponge. pH down is your hands.</p>

<p>pH and alkalinity are not the same number, and mixing them up is the entire problem. pH tells you where your water sits right now. Alkalinity tells you how hard that water will push back. Two houses can have tap water that both read pH 7.4, and one of them will need 1 mL of acid per gallon while the other needs 6. The pH reading is identical. The alkalinity is not even close.</p>

<p>Alkalinity gets measured in meq/L or in ppm of calcium carbonate, and the conversion is friendly: 1 meq/L is about 50 ppm CaCO3. Extension guidance treats roughly 75 ppm, or 1.5 meq/L, as the line where source water starts actively fighting your acid. Below it, your plants and the weak buffering of a nutrient solution do most of the drifting. Above it, you are on a daily acid habit you did not sign up for.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number. In a 52-week nursery trial, researchers acidified irrigation water to strip its alkalinity down from 5 to 3 to 1 meq/L. The water's own pH fell from 7.37 to 6.37 to 4.79, and the growing medium tracked down with it from 6.2 to 5.2 to 4.7. Nobody touched the fertilizer. They just removed the thing that was fighting it.</p>

<p>Two more things push pH in the same direction, and both of them are supposed to happen.</p>

<p>The first is your plants. Standard nutrient formulas are mostly nitrate nitrogen. When a root pulls in a nitrate ion, it has to push a hydroxide or bicarbonate ion back out to stay electrically balanced. A healthy, fast-feeding crop runs that reaction all day long, every day. Rising pH is what a well-fed plant does.</p>

<p>The second is your air stone, and this one genuinely annoyed me when I found out. Bubbling air through a reservoir strips dissolved CO2 out of the water. Removing CO2 raises pH. The pump you bought to keep your roots from drowning is also nudging your pH upward, and it is doing exactly what physics says it should. It is not a fault and there is no part to replace.</p>

